
👉 What Is a Memory Page?
    Memory is not managed byte-by-byte. Instead, it is divided into fixed-size blocks called pages.
    A page is the smallest unit of memory that the OS manages.

👉 What Is Page Size?
    Page size = The size of each memory page.
    Common page sizes:
        4 KB (most common historically)
        8 KB
        16 KB
        64 KB (in some architectures)
        Traditionally, most systems (including Android devices) used 4 KB page size.
        Now, newer systems are moving toward 16 KB page size.

👉 What Is 16 KB Page Size?
    A 16 KB page size means:
        Each memory page = 16 Kilobytes
        1 page = 16 × 1024 bytes = 16384 bytes
    Instead of dividing memory into 4 KB chunks, the OS divides it into 16 KB chunks.

👉 Why Does Page Size Matter?
    Page size directly affects:
    | Area              | Impact                     |
    | ----------------- | -------------------------- |
    | Memory allocation | How memory is reserved     |
    | Performance       | TLB efficiency             |
    | App compatibility | Native libraries alignment |
    | APK behavior      | Binary loading             |
    | System stability  | Memory fragmentation       |

👉 Why Is Android Moving to 16 KB?
    Google has been pushing 16 KB page size support for better performance in newer Android versions and ARM CPUs.
    Modern ARM processors (like ARMv8/ARMv9) are optimized for larger page sizes.

    Benefits include:
    1. Better Performance
        Fewer pages → fewer page table entries → fewer TLB misses.
        (TLB = Translation Lookaside Buffer)
    2. Better Memory Throughput
        Large page size improves:
            Sequential memory access
            App startup speed
            Native library loading
    3. Optimized for Modern Hardware
        Modern CPUs handle larger page sizes more efficiently.

👉 How It Works Internally
    ✔ When your Android app runs:
        App requests memory
        OS allocates memory in pages
        Virtual memory maps to physical memory
        CPU uses page tables
        TLB caches translations
    ✔ If page size is 4 KB:
        More pages needed
        Larger page tables
        More TLB entries
    ✔ If page size is 16 KB:
        Fewer pages
        Smaller page tables
        Better TLB efficiency

👉 Seeing “16 KB Page Size” Errors?
    This happens because: Native libraries (.so files) are built assuming 4 KB alignment.
    If native binaries:
        Are not properly aligned
        Use hardcoded assumptions
        Use old NDK versions
    They may crash on 16 KB devices.

👉 Which Android Versions Support 16 KB?
    Starting from: Android 14
    Google strongly encourages apps to support 16 KB page size.
    Newer devices launching with modern ARM chips may default to 16 KB.

👉 How To Check Page Size on Android
    ✔ Check for Native Libraries
        jniLibs/
        externalNativeBuild
        CMakeLists.txt

    ✔ Check Page Alignment in .so Files
        On Linux/macOS:
            readelf -l libyourlibrary.so | grep LOAD
        Look at alignment values.
            For 16 KB compatibility You can check page size using:
                  getconf PAGE_SIZE Or adb shell getconf PAGE_SIZE
            If output is:
              4096 → 4 KB
              16384 → 16 KB
    ✔ Check Your NDK Version
        In build.gradle: ndkVersion "25.2.9519653"
        Use: NDK r25+ (recommended), Prefer latest stable NDK
        Older NDK versions may not generate compatible binaries.

👉 How To Fix 16 KB Page Size Issues
    ✔ Upgrade NDK
        Update to latest NDK via:
        Android Studio → SDK Manager → SDK Tools → NDK (Side by side)
        Latest Android Studio versions already support this properly.

    ✔ Update CMake Configuration
        In CMakeLists.txt, ensure no forced page size flags exist like: -Wl,-z,max-page-size=4096
        Instead, either remove it or use: -Wl,-z,max-page-size=16384 Or let the toolchain decide automatically (recommended).

    ✔ Rebuild All Native Libraries
        Clean and rebuild:
            ./gradlew clean
            ./gradlew assembleRelease
        Ensure all .so files are regenerated.

    ✔ Update Third-Party Native SDKs
        If you use:
            WebRTC
            FFmpeg
            Payment SDKs
            Streaming SDKs
            Encryption libraries

        You must:
            Contact vendor
            Update to latest version
            Confirm 16 KB page support
        Since you're working with WebRTC and streaming stacks, this is especially critical.

    ✔ Enable Proper ABI Splits
        Ensure targeting: abiFilters "arm64-v8a"
        Most 16 KB devices are ARM64.

    ✔ Test on 16 KB Emulator
        New emulator system images support 16 KB pages.
        Create:
            ARM64 device
            Android 14+
            16 KB page configuration
        Test: adb shell getconf PAGE_SIZE
        If output: 16384

    ✔ Play Store & Future Enforcement
         Google has indicated that future device launches may require 16 KB compatibility.
         Keep monitoring:
             Android Developer Blog
             NDK release notes
             Play Console warnings

    ✔ Architecture-Level Best Practices (For Large Apps)
        CI Validation
            Add script to CI: Scan .so files
            Validate alignment = 0x4000
        Dependency Governance
            Maintain:  Native SDK version tracking and Vendor compatibility matrix
        Avoid Hardcoded Memory Assumptions
            Never: Assume page size = 4096
            Use fixed mmap offsets long pageSize = sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE);

    ✔ Common Crash
              You may see: dlopen failed: unsupported page size

👉 4 KB vs 16 KB Comparison
    | Feature              | 4 KB        | 16 KB                 |
    | -------------------- | ----------- | --------------------- |
    | Memory fragmentation | Higher      | Lower                 |
    | Page table size      | Larger      | Smaller               |
    | TLB efficiency       | Lower       | Higher                |
    | Compatibility        | Legacy safe | Requires modern build |
    | Performance          | Standard    | Better for large apps |


👉 You can also create a script to automatically detect:
The specific native library that is not 16 KB aligned
The ABI (armeabi-v7a, arm64-v8a, x86, etc.) it belongs to
The exact memory alignment value of each ELF segment


